WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.879
i don't know if anyone else has noticed this
but if you look up sodium lauryl sulfate in

00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:09.510
a clean beauty app it's usually rated as clean
but sodium laureth sulfate is usually rated

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:10.510
as dirty

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:15.830
these apps are terrible and based on pseudoscience
sls and sles are both safe in your products

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:20.150
but if you're a skincare nerd you'll know
that sls is considered harsher on your skin

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:23.949
than sles so what's with these ratings?

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:27.970
there's actually a really interesting story
behind this about an innovation that's so

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:33.489
useful it's everywhere in skincare hair care
beauty but i think most people have never

00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:37.929
heard of it i'm michelle of lab muffin beauty
science chemistry phd and cosmetic chemist

00:00:37.929 --> 00:00:42.079
and today i'm going to be talking about the
science of ethoxylation how it turned into

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:46.420
this fear-mongering and why you'll probably
hear a lot more of this fear-mongering over

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:47.800
the next couple of years

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.870
this video is sponsored by croda a cosmetics
ingredient supplier who make tons of ingredients

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.769
that you find in your products both ethoxylated
and non-ethoxylated but as always all the

00:00:56.769 --> 00:01:00.550
ingredients in this video are my own croda
did not supply them

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.070
if you look on the backs of your products
you'll see this in a lot of ingredient names

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:09.560
"eth" a lot of the time not always these three
little letters means that the ingredient is

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.240
ethoxylated there's sodium laureth sulfate
of course but there's also anything that ends

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.250
in eth and a number laureth beheneth cetereth
glycereth

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.490
isethionate ingredients
there are also other ethoxylated ingredients

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:28.270
too without the eth for example anything with
peg and polysorbates all of these ingredients

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:29.580
are ethoxylated

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:34.320
ethoxylation means you take an ingredient
and you add little units containing two carbon

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.550
atoms that's what the eth means in chemistry
and an oxygen which is the oxy part for example

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:45.210
this is the structure of sodium lauryl sulfate
and here is sodium laureth sulfate you can

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:50.340
see the extra added ethoxylation part this
particular sodium laureth sulfate molecule

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.970
has two extra added ethoxyl units these little
units are really important in changing the

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.010
properties of an ingredient

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:02.750
most of these ethoxylated ingredients are
surfactants surfactants have one end that

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:07.690
lacks oil and one end that likes water they're
really important because if you think about

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:13.590
it a lot of skin care and beauty is about
trying to get oil and water to mix which is

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:18.270
the thing that surfactants are best at for
example when you're cleansing your face or

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:22.770
washing your hair you're trying to get oil
to rinse off into water when you put on a

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:28.150
moisturizer you're trying to add oil and water
to your skin in the one product so oil and

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:33.870
water have to hang out and behave together
nicely a lot of fragrances are oily but a

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:38.920
lot of the time you have water-based products
that you might want to make fragranced surfactants

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.610
are key to making all of these work

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:46.700
the main reason ethoxylation is so useful
is that it adds oxygen to the structure oxygen

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.700
is an element with special properties and
the main one is that it can form special bonds

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:57.770
with water called hydrogen bonds so ethoxylation
will make an ingredient like water more the

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:03.250
more of these ethoxy units that you add in
the more water loving it'll be so ethoxylation

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:08.960
is really useful for fine tuning how much
an ingredient prefers water versus oil and

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.480
therefore the specific properties of the overall
product

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.880
the other big impact is that ethoxylation
increases the size of the surfactant and this

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:23.209
means that it doesn't penetrate skin as easily
this makes this effect much more gentle on

00:03:23.209 --> 00:03:29.840
skin and this is why sles is so much gentler
than sls even though they're both sulfates

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.560
one study found that sles could be gentler
than sls as well as a whole bunch of other

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:40.090
non-sulfate surfactants so thanks to ethoxylation
we can have cleansing surfactants that are

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:44.230
gentle on skin but still foam really well
micellar waters with surfactants that can

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:49.010
clean skin effectively but they're so gentle
that you don't need to rinse them off cleansing

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:54.400
balms that apply like an oil but rinse off
cleanly superfatting agents in cleansers shampoos

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.440
and conditioners that act like water-soluble
oils so your skin and hair can be moisturized

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.940
and conditioned while still rinsing clean

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:07.910
but here's the catch ethoxylation is linked
with the chemical 1,4-dioxane 1,4-dioxane

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:11.989
isn't intentionally added to your products
it doesn't have a purpose in beauty but it's

00:04:11.989 --> 00:04:16.930
formed as a by-product during ethoxylation
and it ends up as a trace contaminant it hitches

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:21.289
a ride along with ethoxylated ingredients
that are purposely added to the product

00:04:21.289 --> 00:04:25.539
during the ethoxylation process you're adding
these little ethoxy units with two carbons

00:04:25.539 --> 00:04:31.659
and one oxygen to your ingredient but sometimes
instead of reacting with the ingredient two

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:36.710
ethoxy units will join together and you end
up with this ring and this is 1,4-dioxane

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.949
our by-product

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:44.629
1,4-dioxane is not nice the main concern is
that it's suspected to be a human carcinogen

00:04:44.629 --> 00:04:49.180
even though it hasn't been properly linked
to cancer in humans the evidence that it causes

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:54.870
cancer in animals is strong enough to believe
that it could happen in humans too so it's

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:59.270
probably a good idea not to be exposed to
too much of it there are two ways we can potentially

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:03.710
be exposed to 1,4-dioxane through our beauty
products firstly when we use products with

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:09.330
traces of 1,4-dioxane most of the 1,4-dioxane
evaporates into the air but some of it can

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:13.759
be absorbed through skin and some of it could
be inhaled cleansing products like body wash

00:05:13.759 --> 00:05:19.120
and shampoo have the highest amounts of 1,4-dioxane
not surprising because they also have the

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.610
highest amount of surfactants surfactants
are their active cleansing ingredients

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:28.280
there are also some differences between how
different ingredients are made that changes

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.789
how much 1,4-dioxane ends up in them sles
usually has the highest amounts of contamination

00:05:33.789 --> 00:05:39.069
because of how it's made but the levels of
1,4-dioxane in beauty products have actually

00:05:39.069 --> 00:05:44.349
dropped a lot over the years ingredient manufacturers
have been working on reducing the amounts

00:05:44.349 --> 00:05:49.969
of 1,4-dioxane without there being any laws
in place there are ways to produce the ethoxylated

00:05:49.969 --> 00:05:55.610
ingredients with less 1,4-dioxane and there
are ways to remove a lot of the excess 1,4-dioxane

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.479
afterwards and these have been really effective

00:05:58.479 --> 00:06:02.811
the fda has monitored how much 1,4-dioxane
is in beauty products from the us over the

00:06:02.811 --> 00:06:07.960
years in 1981 the average amount was 50 parts
per million which is equivalent to one drop

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:12.660
in a giant one liter bottle of shampoo by
1997 the average amount had more than dropped

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:19.029
by half to 19 parts per million so a bit less
than half a drop in here in 2008 the fda conducted

00:06:19.029 --> 00:06:24.499
another survey of 35 products the highest
level detected was 11.6 parts per million

00:06:24.499 --> 00:06:28.120
eighty percent of the products had levels
below one part per million which is less than

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:32.669
one fiftieth of a drop in this bottle the
amount considered safe in beauty products

00:06:32.669 --> 00:06:36.930
is slightly different around the world 10
parts per million is the limit recommended

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:41.729
by the scientific committee for consumer safety
in the eu this is the amount that they calculated

00:06:41.729 --> 00:06:47.879
would cause one extra person in 7.5 million
to get cancer per year or the equivalent increased

00:06:47.879 --> 00:06:53.020
lifetime risk of cancer if you smoked half
a cigarette per year for 60 years but like

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:56.990
you saw the vast majority of products don't
have this much anyway so you don't have to

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:58.809
be all ewg about it

00:06:58.809 --> 00:07:03.719
new york recently changed their laws to limit
1,4-dioxane in cosmetics to 10 parts per million

00:07:03.719 --> 00:07:08.499
by the end of 2022 but they've gone further
with household cleaning and personal care

00:07:08.499 --> 00:07:12.439
products the ones that you rinse off your
skin 1,4-dioxane in these will be limited

00:07:12.439 --> 00:07:18.539
to two parts per million by the end of 2022
and one part per million by the end of 2023

00:07:18.539 --> 00:07:24.749
so why do the things that don't stay on our
skin have tighter limits?

00:07:24.749 --> 00:07:30.870
well it's because of the other and overall
bigger concern 1,4-dioxane is a drinking water

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:36.039
contaminant and this is a very real problem
1,4-dioxane absorbs easily from your digestive

00:07:36.039 --> 00:07:40.659
system a lot of places do have quite high
levels of dioxane in their drinking water

00:07:40.659 --> 00:07:47.259
and most places don't have laws on the allowed
level of 1,4-dioxane so the idea is cosmetics

00:07:47.259 --> 00:07:51.849
stay on your body but personal care products
which is defined as cleansing products including

00:07:51.849 --> 00:07:57.270
shampoo conditioner soap and bath products
these go down the drain into the waterways

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.339
which eventually becomes drinking water

00:07:59.339 --> 00:08:05.110
so i guess limiting 1,4-dioxane in personal
care products does make sense in theory but

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:10.759
i'm skeptical it's going to do much 1,4-dioxane
is a trace contaminant in personal care products

00:08:10.759 --> 00:08:15.439
but it's used intentionally in much larger
quantities in other industries it's a solvent

00:08:15.439 --> 00:08:20.139
that's used in paints inks cleaning making
plastics

00:08:20.139 --> 00:08:25.020
1,4-dioxane was most commonly used as an additive
to stabilize chlorinated solvents which were

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:29.680
phased out as part of the montreal protocol
also known as the one time the world actually

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.089
got together and did something about an environmental
disaster the montreal protocol banned the

00:08:34.089 --> 00:08:38.870
use of substances that destroyed the ozone
layer and the main chlorinated solvent that

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:43.949
1,4-dioxane was used with was banned in 1996
so the use of 1,4-dioxane has actually dropped

00:08:43.949 --> 00:08:49.351
massively from its peak in the 80s in the
us it's dropped to about 1/25th of the amount

00:08:49.351 --> 00:08:55.230
used at the peak but every year about 1 million
pounds which is half a million kilograms if

00:08:55.230 --> 00:09:00.959
you're in a country that uses real numbers
that much dioxane is used every year and in

00:09:00.959 --> 00:09:08.509
2016 62 000 pounds which is 28 000 kilograms
of that was released into the water

00:09:08.509 --> 00:09:11.870
so we can do a calculation to work out how
much shower gel and shampoo you'd have to

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:16.399
use to release that amount of dioxane into
the water if your products have an average

00:09:16.399 --> 00:09:21.910
of two parts per million every single person
in the US would have to rinse 43.5 liters

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:27.800
of beauty products down the drain every year
that is about one-third of this mug or 24

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:32.690
palm-sized dollops of product every day and
even then these amounts that are currently

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:37.490
released by industry every year aren't thought
to be the biggest contributor to 1,4-dioxane

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:41.920
in drinking water the amounts of 1,4-dioxane
used decades ago before chlorinated solvents

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:47.350
were phased out were much much higher there
are lots of old industrial areas and improperly

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:52.449
lined disposal sites that are contaminated
with 1,4-dioxane and this can leach into underground

00:09:52.449 --> 00:09:55.259
water which is where a lot of our drinking
water comes from

00:09:55.259 --> 00:10:01.450
a 2017 study found that dioxane in contaminated
water was correlated with chlorinated solvent

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:07.379
levels and this points towards the old industrial
uses as the main culprit in new york where

00:10:07.379 --> 00:10:12.329
this law is being introduced supposedly to
improve drinking water it's long island that

00:10:12.329 --> 00:10:16.690
has particularly contaminated water and it's
thought that it's because there are lots of

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:21.610
old industrial sites there and their drinking
water comes from underground aquifers so to

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:26.440
me it seems like a much better approach would
be to treat the industrial sites either way

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:30.069
these new limits are coming in at the end
of the year in new york

00:10:30.069 --> 00:10:34.510
so i think this whole thing is a really interesting
story that highlights a lot of themes that

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.860
come up a lot in the beauty discourse

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:43.079
firstly really amazing innovations can become
a victim of their own success things that

00:10:43.079 --> 00:10:47.629
get really popular also get really scrutinized
and if there's any sort of fearmongering that

00:10:47.629 --> 00:10:52.319
can be done you know that certain sections
of the beauty industry are going to try their

00:10:52.319 --> 00:10:54.019
best to take advantage of it

00:10:54.019 --> 00:10:59.060
crystal ball time i think we're probably going
to see some 1,4-dioxane scare stories around

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:03.320
the end of the year when these restrictions
start taking place which will be a great christmas

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:04.320
present for me

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:09.240
i also think that other places will make similar
changes because theme number two sometimes

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:14.279
the easy things get regulated and the hard
but more important things get ignored sometimes

00:11:14.279 --> 00:11:19.330
maybe lawmakers genuinely don't understand
the issue but sometimes this can be a strategic

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:24.639
decision to look like they're doing something
it acts as a smoke screen for inaction setting

00:11:24.639 --> 00:11:29.100
a limit that manufacturers have to meet is
cheap and easy but remediating industrial

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:33.770
sites is a lot harder new york is going to
evaluate whether this law actually worked

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:39.199
in 2025 and i do think they'll say it did
because dioxane's actually been going down

00:11:39.199 --> 00:11:43.389
anyway plus scientists are developing lots
of ways of getting dioxane out of wastewater

00:11:43.389 --> 00:11:47.701
and drinking water i hope someone will do
an analysis to see if the personal care product

00:11:47.701 --> 00:11:50.579
limit will make a difference because i could
be wrong

00:11:50.579 --> 00:11:55.089
plus the beauty industry does proactively
do good things without being forced to by

00:11:55.089 --> 00:11:59.940
specific regulations the catch is that a lot
of it does depend on what they feel like consumers

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:04.350
want so we need to be asking for the right
things not things that are based on misunderstandings

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:05.819
